NVIDIA and Amazon meet in one sample router, and the library's server version is 0.3.0.

00:06:01.946 --> 00:06:07.626
Nimo Switchyard is what Amazon's post uses to pick which Amazon bedrock model answers

00:06:07.626 --> 00:06:08.566
a coding agent.

00:06:08.926 --> 00:06:16.246
The sample installs that server from crates.io and runs Light LLM image version 1.102.0.

00:06:16.646 --> 00:06:21.806
Both in one container on elastic container service on Fargate, in private subnets.

00:06:22.406 --> 00:06:27.646
Amazon says Nvidia documents that release as a demo and evaluation piece, and Amazon

00:06:27.646 --> 00:06:32.046
calls the Integration Experimental Sample Code, not a production deployment.

00:06:32.666 --> 00:06:35.646
A coding agent works in tasks, not in single calls.

00:06:36.186 --> 00:06:37.906
The developer asks for a change.

00:06:38.426 --> 00:06:42.306
The agent reads files, edits code, and runs tests until the work is done.

00:06:42.966 --> 00:06:45.426
One task can take a few model calls or dozens.

00:06:46.126 --> 00:06:50.666
Token price is not task price because the bill depends on how many turns a model needs,

00:06:51.166 --> 00:06:56.166
how much history each turn replays, and how often a run is retried or never finishes.

00:06:56.166 --> 00:07:01.646
images. Every attempt is paid, including the ones that never finish. Amazon's score is

00:07:01.646 --> 00:07:06.926
cost-per-solve task. Total cost of the runs divided by the number of runs whose tests

00:07:06.926 --> 00:07:13.626
pass. Two strategies sit in the light LLM integration. Stage routing reads recent tool activity

00:07:13.626 --> 00:07:18.926
and results, so the agent has to send the full conversation, then picks a capable model

00:07:18.926 --> 00:07:24.366
or a lower cost-efficient one. Random routing picks from the configured models, with optional

00:07:24.366 --> 00:07:28.946
waits for splits and comparisons. Neither strategy calls a judge model.

00:07:29.606 --> 00:07:34.026
Light LLM has also shipped a native Switchyard stage router as an auto router.

00:07:34.546 --> 00:07:40.246
The sample deploys with the AWS cloud development kit. Amazon tells customers to clear it with

00:07:40.246 --> 00:07:46.166
security and legal teams and to test in a non-production account first. Amazon hosts the gateway

00:07:46.166 --> 00:07:51.926
and the models. Nvidia supplies the chooser. The agent sends an open AI compatible request

00:07:51.926 --> 00:07:55.626
with the model field set to switch yard and a shared gateway key.

00:07:56.206 --> 00:08:03.526
Only the IPv4 range the deployer allows can reach the host name on a load balancer with a certificate manager certificate.

00:08:04.106 --> 00:08:10.266
AWS WF applies IP reputation, known bad input and per IP rate limits.

00:08:10.846 --> 00:08:16.066
The load balancer forwards only three paths, chat completions, models, and a health check,

00:08:16.466 --> 00:08:18.866
and returns a 404 for every other path.

00:08:19.466 --> 00:08:24.446
Light LLM checks the key, the plug-in picks the model, and the call goes through bedrock

00:08:24.446 --> 00:08:29.806
converse or converse stream on the task's own identity role via a runtime interface

00:08:29.806 --> 00:08:32.886
endpoint, so inference stays on Amazon's network.

00:08:33.546 --> 00:08:37.366
The header xLiteLLM model name names the model that answered.

00:08:37.966 --> 00:08:42.846
Amazon gets a harness that compares a routed group with single model baselines on a customer's

00:08:42.846 --> 00:08:46.206
own workloads, which is how the post says to judge the router.

00:08:46.206 --> 00:08:51.906
And Vidya gets the library in the path of agents that already speak a common format, without

00:08:51.906 --> 00:08:55.106
